  "query_preview": "(This Evening through Monday)...\nIssued at 138 PM EDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nDry and pleasant weather continues through midweek as central \nIndiana resides under the influence of high pressure. Deep upper \nlevel ridging will remain tethered for the next several days over \nthe Intermountain West with a largely dry northwest flow over the \nregion. The passage of a cold front late Wednesday into Thursday \nwill present the primary convective risk through the forecast \nperiod. The expansion of upper level ridging by late weekend will \nbring the return of a hotter and more humid airmass to the Ohio \nValley into early next week.\n\nRest of This Afternoon through Wednesday \n\nAnother beautiful late summer afternoon in progress as the region \nresides under high pressure. A scattered diurnal cu field has \ndeveloped with generally light northwest winds. 17Z temperatures \nwere in the 70s with comfortable humidity levels. \n\nA superb next 48 hours through Wednesday afternoon is in store as \nthe surface high drifts slowly through the region and remains the \nprimary influence across the Ohio Valley. A brief period with \nincreased mid and high clouds may graze the lower Wabash Valley this \nevening as a weak upper level wave passes through the lower Ohio \nValley. Otherwise partly cloudy skies are expected through Wednesday \nwith scattered cu developing each afternoon and diminishing near or \njust after sunset. With mainly clear skies and light winds...patchy \nfog will be likely late tonight and Tuesday morning in the favored \nareas in valleys and near streams. \n\nAs return flow develops from the southwest an increase in low level \nmoisture will lead to an increase in cu coverage Wednesday afternoon \nin advance of a cold front approaching from the northwest. The cold \nfront will approach in tandem with an upper level low rounding the \ncrest of the ridge aloft to our west and dropping across the Great \nLakes on Wednesday. Convection will generate to our north by late \nday in advance of the cold front.\n\nTemps...low level thermals support highs largely in the low and mid \n80s Tuesday and Wednesday with the warmer day on Wednesday courtesy \nof the advent of the southwesterly flow. Another night with lows in \nthe 50s tonight will be followed by a moderating trend back into the \n60s by midweek.\n\nWednesday Night through Monday\n\nThe aforementioned upper level low will become absorbed  becoming \nabsorbed by a broader long wave trough Wednesday into Thursday that \nwill amplify across the eastern half of the country for late week. \nLow pressure and an associated cold front running in tandem with the \nupper wave will track across the Ohio Valley Wednesday night and \nearly Thursday in a progressively weakened condition as stronger \nforcing aloft is lost. Stronger convection that develops over lower \nMichigan and northern Illinois by late day Wednesday along the \nboundary may make it into far northern counties late Wednesday night \nin a weakened state before coverage drops off in the unfavorable \npredawn hours of Thursday with minimal instability available. The \nfront will progress through the forecast area Thursday with \nscattered redevelopment over southern parts of the forecast area in \nthe afternoon before clearing the area late day.\n\nHigh pressure will reestablish in the wake of the frontal passage \ndrifting slowly east through the Great Lakes into Saturday. This \nbring another multi day stretch of dry weather with seasonable \ntemperatures as highs will focus largely in the low to mid 80s. \nReturn flow develops on Sunday as the high shifts off to the east... \nwith an increase in temperatures and humidity levels by early next \nweek as the ridge aloft to the west expands towards the Ohio Valley. \nThe increase in moisture and instability will lead to an \nintroduction of isolated to scattered mainly afternoon and evening \nconvection. Highs in the upper 80s and lower 90s are expected by \nSunday and Monday with max heat indices into the upper 90s.",
